=[ tomet ]

Generated by `tomet cli-docs` from the clap definitions in `apps/cli/src/cli.rs`. Do not edit it: change the doc comments there and run the command again.

Parse and inspect Tomet (.tmt) files

```text
tomet <COMMAND>
```

==[ tomet check ]

Check a `.tmt` file, or every one under a directory, and report whether it is valid: it parses, and every element it writes exists in a namespace the document has in scope.

A directory is swept the way `format --check` and `check-links` sweep one -- through the workspace index, so `workspace.ignore` applies and hidden files like `.writ.tmt` are included. One unreadable file does not stop the sweep; the run exits non-zero at the end.

```text
tomet check [OPTIONS] [PATH]
```

Arguments:

- `[PATH]`: File or directory to check (defaults to ".").

Options:

- `--data`: Parse as a data-only document (key: value / seq / scalar) instead of the full document grammar. One file only -- a directory of data-only documents is not a thing this has met.
- `-q, --quiet`: Do not print "OK" when parse succeeds.
- `--json`: Output parse diagnostic error as JSON format.

==[ tomet stats ]

Print statistics for a `.tmt` file, or every one under a directory: how many characters, elements, links and headings it holds.

Characters are the prose a reader sees -- text inside `[...]`, headings included, whitespace and code block bodies left out. `(args)` and `{value}` are data and are not counted. Elements are split by sigil (named, `${}`, `^`, and list items) and the named ones by name. Links are the ones `check-links` looks at. A directory is swept the way `check` sweeps one, and an unreadable file does not stop the sweep; the run exits non-zero at the end.

```text
tomet stats [OPTIONS] [PATH]
```

Arguments:

- `[PATH]`: File or directory to measure (defaults to ".").

Options:

- `--json`: Print the statistics as JSON instead of human-readable text.

==[ tomet ast ]

Parse a file and pretty-print the resulting AST

```text
tomet ast [OPTIONS] <FILE>
```

Arguments:

- `<FILE>`

Options:

- `--data`

==[ tomet roundtrip ]

Parse a data file into a generic value, then render it back out through `tove` and reparse that -- confirms the save/load round trip is lossless

```text
tomet roundtrip <FILE>
```

Arguments:

- `<FILE>`

==[ tomet html ]

Convert a `.tmt` file to HTML: a standalone page, or just the rendered body with `--body`

```text
tomet html [OPTIONS] <FILE>
```

Arguments:

- `<FILE>`

Options:

- `-o, --out <OUT>`: Write to this path instead of stdout.
- `--advanced`: Number headings sequentially by nesting level (1, 1.1, 1.2, 2, ...) instead of the plain, unnumbered default.
- `--lang <LANG>`: `<html lang="...">` for the page shell. Defaults to "ja".
- `--body`: Emit just the rendered body, with no `<html>`/`<head>` shell, for templating into a page of your own. `--lang` has no effect alongside this, since the shell is what carries it.

==[ tomet to-md ]

Convert a `.tmt` file to CommonMark. Lossy for constructs with no Markdown equivalent (`@links{}`, and any `@T` element the importer never produces); `tomet-markdown`'s module docs list what falls back to HTML passthrough

```text
tomet to-md [OPTIONS] <FILE>
```

Arguments:

- `<FILE>`

Options:

- `-o, --out <OUT>`: Write to this path instead of stdout.

==[ tomet to-typst ]

Convert a `.tmt` file to Typst markup source. Lossy for constructs with no Typst equivalent -- see `tomet-convert-typst`'s crate doc

```text
tomet to-typst [OPTIONS] <FILE>
```

Arguments:

- `<FILE>`

Options:

- `-o, --out <OUT>`: Write to this path instead of stdout.

==[ tomet to-pandoc ]

Convert a `.tmt` file to Pandoc's JSON AST, which `pandoc -f json` reads -- the bridge to every format Pandoc writes (docx, LaTeX, EPUB, org, ...). Pass `-` to read the `.tmt` from stdin

```text
tomet to-pandoc [OPTIONS] <FILE>
```

Arguments:

- `<FILE>`

Options:

- `-o, --out <OUT>`: Write to this path instead of stdout.

==[ tomet from-pandoc ]

Convert Pandoc's JSON AST (`pandoc -t json <file>`) to Tomet (.tmt) -- the bridge from every format Pandoc reads. Pass `-` to read the JSON from stdin, which is the usual way: `pandoc -t json x.docx | tomet from-pandoc -`

```text
tomet from-pandoc [OPTIONS] <FILE>
```

Arguments:

- `<FILE>`: Target `.json` file, or `-` for stdin.

Options:

- `-o, --out <OUT>`: Write to this path instead of stdout.

==[ tomet from-md ]

Convert CommonMark Markdown (.md) to Tomet (.tmt)

```text
tomet from-md [OPTIONS] <PATH>
```

Arguments:

- `<PATH>`: Target .md file or directory containing .md files.

Options:

- `-o, --out <OUT>`: Write to this path instead of stdout (for single file) or target output directory (for directory).
- `-i, --in-place`: Write converted `.tmt` file(s) in place.
- `--remove-original`: Remove original `.md` file(s) after conversion (only with --in-place or --out).
- `--dry-run`: Dry-run mode: convert and validate in memory without writing files.

==[ tomet serve ]

Serve a `.tmt` file as HTML over HTTP on 127.0.0.1, re-rendering it fresh on every request (just reload the page after editing)

```text
tomet serve [OPTIONS] <FILE>
```

Arguments:

- `<FILE>`

Options:

- `-p, --port <PORT>` Default: `8787`.
- `--advanced`: Number headings sequentially by nesting level (1, 1.1, 1.2, 2, ...) instead of the plain, unnumbered default.
- `--lang <LANG>`: `<html lang="...">` for the page shell. Defaults to "ja".

==[ tomet format ]

Normalize a `.tmt`/`.tmt` file's whitespace (line endings, trailing whitespace, blank lines, final newline). A directory formats every `.tmt`/`.tmt` file found under it (same file discovery as `export`/ `check-links`: honors `.gitignore` and the project config's `ignore_files`). A file named explicitly is held to the same rule: one the config excludes (`workspace.ignore`/`workspace.unswept`, e.g. the frozen test corpus) is skipped with a warning unless `--force` is given. Prints to stdout by default; see `--in-place`/`--check`

```text
tomet format [OPTIONS] <PATHS>...
```

Arguments:

- `<PATHS>...`: Target file(s) or directory/directories to format.

Options:

- `-i, --in-place`: Overwrite the file(s) in place instead of printing to stdout.
- `--check`: Exit with a nonzero status if any file isn't already formatted, without writing or printing anything. Conflicts with `--in-place`.
- `--force`: Format files and directories the project config excludes (`workspace.ignore`, `workspace.unswept`) as well. `.gitignore` still applies.

==[ tomet cli-docs ]

Write the CLI reference -- every subcommand with its usage, arguments and options -- as a `.tmt`, generated from the clap definitions. Prints to stdout unless `--out` is given; with `--check` it exits non-zero when that file is not what would be written

```text
tomet cli-docs [OPTIONS]
```

Options:

- `-o, --out <OUT>`: Write to this file instead of stdout.
- `--check`: Fail if `--out`'s file is missing or out of date, without writing.

==[ tomet tui ]

Launch the interactive TUI workbench for Markdown migration, batch metadata editing, and structural AST refactoring

```text
tomet tui [OPTIONS] [PATH]
```

Arguments:

- `[PATH]`: Target directory or file path (defaults to current directory ".").

Options:

- `-c, --config <CONFIG>`: Optional path to custom formatting configuration file (.tmt).

==[ tomet export ]

Export a `.tmt` document or directory of documents based on `@config` settings or CLI overrides

```text
tomet export [OPTIONS] [PATH]
```

Arguments:

- `[PATH]`: Target file or directory path (defaults to current directory ".").

Options:

- `-t, --type <TYPE>`: Target format (`commonmark` | `html` | `all`). Overrides `@config`.
- `-o, --out <OUT>`: Output destination file or directory path. Overrides `@config`.
- `--advanced`: Number headings sequentially by nesting level for HTML export.
- `--check`: Compare each declared output against what the source renders to now, without writing anything. Exits non-zero if any differ or are missing -- a generated file that disagrees with its source is a failure with a mechanical fix.

==[ tomet check-links ]

Check every `@file`/`<embed>` link in a `.tmt`/`.tmt` document or directory for broken (non-existent) local-file targets. Uses an SQLite cache (keyed by source-file mtime) in the user's cache directory -- `~/.cache/tomet/` on Unix, never inside the vault -- so re-checking a large vault doesn't re-parse unchanged files. Exits non-zero if any broken link is found

```text
tomet check-links [OPTIONS] [PATH]
```

Arguments:

- `[PATH]`: Target file or directory path (defaults to current directory ".").

Options:

- `--json`: Print the report as JSON instead of human-readable text.

==[ tomet refactor ]

Refactor .tmt file(s) across a workspace (URL macros, `@meta.type` -> `@kind`, Value DSL normalization)

```text
tomet refactor [OPTIONS] [PATH]
```

Arguments:

- `[PATH]`: Target file or directory path (defaults to current directory ".").

Options:

- `-i, --in-place`: Write modified files in place.
- `--url-macros`: Only rewrite URLs matching macros.
- `--meta-kind`: Only promote `@meta.type` to `@kind`.
- `--value-dsl`: Only normalize `@meta(format:yaml)` to Value DSL.
- `--check`: Check/dry-run mode without modifying files (exits non-zero if changes are needed). Conflicts with `--in-place`.
- `--force`: Refactor files and directories the project config excludes (`workspace.ignore`, `workspace.unswept`) as well. Without it, a file named explicitly that the config excludes is skipped with a warning, as it would be in a directory sweep.

==[ tomet new ]

Create a new .tmt document from a blueprint

```text
tomet new [OPTIONS] [PATH]
```

Arguments:

- `[PATH]`: Target file path to create (defaults to "document.tmt" if omitted when --list is not used).

Options:

- `-b, --blueprint <BLUEPRINT>`: Blueprint name, as the blueprint gives it in `@blueprint(...)` (e.g. "daily-note", "rfc"). Resolved among the blueprints the vault declares in `blueprints`, never searched for on disk.
- `-l, --list`: List the blueprints this vault declares instead of creating a file.
- `-f, --force`: Overwrite target file if it already exists.
- `--var <VARS>`: Pass custom variable as `key=value`. Can be specified multiple times.
